## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin architecture for critical control logic. It tests understanding of extensibility patterns, safety considerations, and observability requirements under high-load production environments similar to those at Netflix, Tesla, or Oracle.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy Pattern**: Encapsulates each evaluation algorithm (Boolean, Percentage, UserSegment) behind a common interface
- **Registry/Plugin Loader**: Maintains a map of evaluator names to implementations, enabling runtime registration without redeployment
- **Hot-Reloadable Modules**: Allows new evaluators to be added or updated without restarting the service
- **Per-Flag Isolation**: Each flag has dedicated configuration and metrics to prevent cascade failures
- **Observability**: Built-in logging, metrics, and audit trails for every flag evaluation

## Code Example
```typescript
interface FlagEvaluationContext {
  userId?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, any>;
  [key: string]: any;
}

interface FlagConfig {
  key: string;
  strategy: string;
  parameters?: Record<string, any>;
  enabled?: boolean;
}

interface IFlagEvaluator {
  name: string;
  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, context: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean;
}

class FlagRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();
  private cache = new Map<string, IFlagEvaluator>();

  register(evaluator: IFlagEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator);
    this.cache.delete(evaluator.name);
  }

  get(name: string): IFlagEvaluator | undefined {
    if (!this.cache.has(name)) {
      this.cache.set(name, this.evaluators.get(name));
    }
    return this.cache.get(name);
  }

  evaluate(config: FlagConfig, ctx: FlagEvaluationContext): boolean {
    const evaluator = this.get(config.strategy);
    if (!evaluator) throw new Error(`Unknown strategy: ${config.strategy}`);
    return evaluator.evaluate(config, ctx);
  }
}

// Built-in evaluators
const BooleanEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Boolean',
  evaluate: (config) => config.parameters?.value === true,
};

const PercentageEvaluator: IFlagEvaluator = {
  name: 'Percentage',
  evaluate: (config, ctx) => {
    const percentage = config.parameters?.percentage ?? 0;
    const hash = this.hash(ctx.userId ?? Math.random().toString());
    return (hash % 100) < percentage;
  },
  hash: (str: string) => str.split('').reduce((a, b) => ((a << 5) - a) + b.charCodeAt(0), 0),
};

// Usage
const registry = new FlagRegistry();
registry.register(BooleanEvaluator);
registry.register(PercentageEvaluator);

const result = registry.evaluate(
  { key: 'new-feature', strategy: 'Percentage', parameters: { percentage: 25 } },
  { userId: 'user-123' }
);
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores designing a runtime-extensible policy engine supporting hot-pluggable evaluators, multi-tenancy, and auditable decisions—realistic requirements for large-scale data platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime registries and factories for pluggable evaluators
- Policy pipelines with per-tenant and per-resource scoping
- Chain of Responsibility pattern for sequential evaluation with short-circuiting
- Decision caching with invalidation on policy changes
- Precedence rules: explicit Deny overrides Allow; evaluators run in registered order unless overridden per resource

## Code Example
```typescript
interface EvaluationContext {
  tenant: string
  user: string
  resource: string
  action: string
  headers?: Record<string, string>
}

type Decision = 'Allow' | 'Deny'

interface EvaluationResult {
  decision: Decision
  evaluator: string
  reason?: string
}

interface IPolicyEvaluator {
  name: string
  evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult>
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private evaluators = new Map<string, IPolicyEvaluator>()

  register(evaluator: IPolicyEvaluator): void {
    this.evaluators.set(evaluator.name, evaluator)
  }

  get(name: string): IPolicyEvaluator | undefined {
    return this.evaluators.get(name)
  }

  getAll(): IPolicyEvaluator[] {
    return Array.from(this.evaluators.values())
  }
}

class PolicyPipeline {
  constructor(
    private registry: PolicyRegistry,
    private pipelineConfig: Map<string, string[]>
  ) {}

  async evaluate(ctx: EvaluationContext): Promise<EvaluationResult> {
    const evaluatorNames = this.pipelineConfig.get(ctx.resource) 
      ?? this.pipelineConfig.get('default') 
      ?? ['TenantRule', 'IPAllowlist', 'TimeWindow', 'DeviceTrust']

    for (const name of evaluatorNames) {
      const evaluator = this.registry.get(name)
      if (!evaluator) continue

      const result = await evaluator.evaluate(ctx)
      if (result.decision === 'Deny') {
        return result
      }
    }

    return { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'pipeline' }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry()
registry.register({ name: 'TenantRule', evaluate: async (ctx) => 
  ctx.tenant ? { decision: 'Allow', evaluator: 'TenantRule' } 
             : { decision: 'Deny', evaluator: 'TenantRule', reason: 'Missing tenant' }
})

const pipeline = new PolicyPipeline(registry, new Map([['default', ['TenantRule']]]))
const result = await pipeline.evaluate({ tenant: 'acme', user: 'alice', resource: 'file', action: 'read' })
```

